{"title":"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 19","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A regular pentagon has all sides and angles equal. If the shaded pentagon is enclosed by squares and triangles, as shown, what is the size of angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />?\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p><i>An image is supposed to go here.  You can help us out by creating one and <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit\">editing it in</a></span>.  Thanks.</i>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/3/573e9bbb49fcf5cc4d7f4158878d555833bf46d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)} 75^{\\circ} \\quad\\text{(B)} 108^{\\circ} \\quad \\text{(C)} 90^{\\circ} \\quad \\text{(D)} 60^{\\circ} \\quad \\text{(E)} 72^{\\circ} \\quad$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"348\" height=\"19\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can realize that the measurement in degrees of each of the angles in the squares is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/7/6a7dfa8a2a90d521b2b03f5b82a1fb9f85dce88c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$90^{\\circ}$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" /> and that each of the angles in the regular pentagon is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/8/cd8060e5b4b3905a244e7bdb17ac8b6a92a3071b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$108^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" /> because of the formula for the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polygon#Interior\" title=\"Polygon\">sum of the interior angles</a> in a polygon and the angles being equal in the shapes.\n</p><p>We also can recognize that a full revolution would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/b/96bd59024f5cb49b070ca9b0f8906499940c75e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$360^{\\circ}$\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />. Combining everything together, we have:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/e/27e9b0b2577704830cfd35a559fbf8564c5d3e8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x + 90^{\\circ} + 90^{\\circ} + 108^{\\circ} = 360^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"225\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/9/479a177adac44e9575361d35c1aef12afa6a97b4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x + 288^{\\circ} = 360^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"128\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/d/4fdbd2908c7b9bb3b3eda5f90302dd3c62b789b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 360^{\\circ} - 288^{\\circ} = \\boxed {\\textbf {(E) } 72^{\\circ}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"224\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)\">1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems&amp;acti